    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "### Describe the bug\r\n\r\nTrying to use `type` class object type hint doesn't seem to work with compiled Python code:\r\n\r\n```\r\n$ python3 -c \"import test4\"\r\nTraceback (most recent call last):\r\n  File \"<string>\", line 1, in <module>\r\n  File \"test4.py\", line 4, in init test4\r\n    FooType = type[Element]\r\nTypeError: 'type' object is not subscriptable\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhere test4.py has been compiled into a .so library.\r\n\r\n### Code to reproduce the behaviour:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nclass Element: pass\r\n\r\nFooType = type[Element]\r\n# OriginalType = defaultdict[type[Element], list[DifferentClass]]\r\n```\r\n\r\ncompiled with:\r\n```\r\ncython -Wextra -X always_allow_keywords=True -3 -o test4.c test4.py\r\ngcc -fPIC -shared -lpython3.11 -o test4.so test4.c\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Expected behaviour\r\n\r\nNo error, ideally. Python3.11 is fine with it normally:\r\n\r\n```\r\n$ python3\r\nPython 3.11.4 (main, Jul 21 2023, 09:59:36) [GCC 10.2.1 20210130 (Red Hat 10.2.1-11)] on linux\r\nType \"help\", \"copyright\", \"credits\" or \"license\" for more information.\r\n>>> class Element: pass\r\n...\r\n>>> FooType = type[Element]\r\n>>>\r\n```\r\n\r\n\r\n### OS\r\n\r\nLinux\r\n\r\n### Python version\r\n\r\n3.11.4\r\n\r\n### Cython version\r\n\r\n3.0.0\r\n\r\n### Additional context\r\n\r\n_No response_",
